cff-version: 1.2.0
title: >-
  who-decides: code and papers on who votes and who funds elections in
  Washington, New York, Texas, and Idaho
message: >-
  If you use this repository or its papers, please cite the archived release.
  Once a Zenodo DOI is minted, add it under `identifiers` below and replace the
  repository link in each paper with the versioned DOI.
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Kirby
    given-names: Stephen
    affiliation: Tikor Consulting
    email: kirby@tikorconsulting.com
repository-code: 'https://github.com/skirby359/who-decides'
abstract: >-
  Open-source reproduction code and papers measuring who returns the ballot and
  who funds campaigns across four U.S. states. Individual registration and vote
  records are linked to itemized campaign contributions to compare the age and
  partisan composition of electorates against the donor class that finances
  them. The repository ships verification scripts that re-derive every published
  headline number from scratch; the underlying voter files are not
  redistributable and are not included.
keywords:
  - voter turnout
  - off-cycle elections
  - election timing
  - campaign finance
  - political donors
  - record linkage
  - electoral competitiveness
  - Washington
  - New York
  - Idaho
  - Texas
license: MIT
license-url: 'https://github.com/skirby359/who-decides/blob/master/LICENSE'
# Papers in docs/ are released under CC-BY 4.0, not MIT. See README "License".

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# HUMAN STEP — minting the archival DOI (the last open pre-submission item).
#
#   1. Sign in to https://zenodo.org with the GitHub account and enable the
#      skirby359/who-decides repository on the Zenodo GitHub page.
#   2. Tag and publish a GitHub release (e.g. v1.0.0). Zenodo archives the
#      tagged tree and mints both a version DOI and a concept DOI.
#   3. Put the CONCEPT DOI in `identifiers` below and set `version` /
#      `date-released` to the tagged release.
#   4. Replace the bare repository link in each paper's byline block with the
#      versioned DOI, so a reader cites an immutable tree rather than a moving
#      branch. The papers currently link the repository without a commit pin,
#      which is the specific gap this file exists to close.
#
# Enabling the repository on Zenodo BEFORE tagging is required — Zenodo only
# archives releases created after the hook is switched on.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# version: 1.0.0
# date-released: 'YYYY-MM-DD'
# identifiers:
#   - type: doi
#     value: 10.5281/zenodo.XXXXXXX
#     description: Concept DOI (always resolves to the latest version)
